## Basic `try...catch`

```js
try {
  JSON.parse("not valid json");
} catch (error) {
  console.error("Something went wrong:", error.message);
}
```

Any exception thrown inside `try` jumps to `catch`, with the thrown value bound to the parameter.

## Adding `finally`

`finally` runs whether or not an exception occurred — useful for cleanup:

```js
let connection;
try {
  connection = openConnection();
  doWork(connection);
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error);
} finally {
  connection?.close(); // always runs
}
```

## Optional catch binding

If you don't need the error object (ES2019+):

```js
try {
  riskyThing();
} catch {
  console.log("failed, moving on");
}
```

## Catching specific error types

JavaScript has no typed catch clauses, so you branch inside:

```js
try {
  doStuff();
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof TypeError) {
    // handle type errors
  } else if (error instanceof RangeError) {
    // handle range errors
  } else {
    throw error; // rethrow anything you can't handle
  }
}
```

**Rethrowing unknown errors is important** — swallowing everything hides real bugs.

## Async code

`try...catch` works with `await`:

```js
async function loadUser(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Failed to load user:", error);
    return null;
  }
}
```

But it does **not** catch errors from promises you don't await, or from callbacks that run later:

```js
// ❌ catch never fires — the error happens after try block exits
try {
  setTimeout(() => { throw new Error("boom"); }, 100);
} catch (e) {
  console.log("never reached");
}
```

For raw promises, use `.catch()`:

```js
fetch("/api/data")
  .then(res => res.json())
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```

## Throwing your own errors

Always throw `Error` objects (or subclasses), not strings — you get a stack trace:

```js
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message, field) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ValidationError";
    this.field = field;
  }
}

try {
  throw new ValidationError("Email is required", "email");
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof ValidationError) {
    console.log(error.field); // "email"
  }
}
```

## Preserving the original error

Use the `cause` option (ES2022) when wrapping:

```js
try {
  await saveToDb(record);
} catch (error) {
  throw new Error("Could not save record", { cause: error });
}
```

